---
title: 'CI Configuration File'
description: 'Configure Nx Cloud CI and external CI providers with the .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml file: workers, launch templates, Ultracache, and self-healing.'
filter: 'type:References'
---

Nx Cloud reads CI configuration from a `.nx/pipelines/ci.yaml` file in your workspace.
The same file format works whether your pipeline runs on [Nx Cloud CI](/docs/features/ci-features#run-ci-on-nx-cloud) or on your own CI provider, such as GitHub Actions.

{% aside type="note" title="Using .nx/ci-config.yaml?" %}
We've added support for `.nx/pipelines/ci.yaml`. See the [migration guide](/docs/kb/migrate-from-ci-config).
{% /aside %}

{% tabs syncKey="ci-location" %}
{% tabitem label="Bring your own CI" %}

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
worker-templates:
  default:
    workers: 5 linux-large-js
    stop-after:
      - build
      - test
```

Start the workers once, early in the main job of your provider's workflow:

```shell
npx nx-cloud start-workers
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="Nx Cloud CI" %}

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
on:
  pull-requests:
    event: PR
  main-pushes:
    event: PUSH
    branches: [main]

worker-templates:
  default:
    workers: 5 linux-large-js
    stop-after:
      - build
      - test

jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: linux-medium-js
    workers: default
    steps:
      - run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% /tabs %}

## Top-level structure

The file serves two setups: **Bring your own CI**, where your own CI provider such as GitHub Actions runs the pipeline, and **Nx Cloud CI**, where Nx Cloud runs it.
The **Used by** column shows which setup uses each section. `Both` means the section applies to either one.

| Section            | Purpose                                              | Used by     |
| ------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------- | ----------- |
| `worker-templates` | Named worker setups: counts, sizes, and task rules.  | Both        |
| `launch-templates` | Machine setups that workers and jobs run on.         | Both        |
| `ultracache`       | Paths that Ultracache ignores.                       | Both        |
| `self-healing`     | Which tasks self-healing CI can fix.                 | Both        |
| `name`             | Display name of the pipeline.                        | Nx Cloud CI |
| `on`               | Events that start the jobs.                          | Nx Cloud CI |
| `env`              | Environment variables for every job and its workers. | Nx Cloud CI |
| `defaults`         | Defaults for the steps in every job.                 | Nx Cloud CI |
| `jobs`             | The jobs and their steps.                            | Nx Cloud CI |

Your own CI provider ignores the Nx Cloud CI sections, so one file can configure both. There, workers get environment variables from their launch template's `env` and from [`start-workers --forward-secrets` and `--forward-vars`](/docs/reference/nx-cloud-cli#nx-cloud-start-workers).

{% aside type="note" title="File name" %}
Nx Cloud CI runs every `.yaml` or `.yml` file in `.nx/pipelines` that has an `on` section, so you can name the file something other than `ci.yaml`.
{% /aside %}

For editor validation, add the published schema to the first line of the file:

```yaml
# yaml-language-server: $schema=https://cloud.nx.app/nx-cloud/static/schemas/workflow.schema.json
```

## `worker-templates`

Named worker setups, at most 50 entries.
On your own CI provider, [`start-workers`](/docs/reference/nx-cloud-cli#nx-cloud-start-workers) starts one entry.
On Nx Cloud CI, a job picks one with `workers: <name>`.

An entry is a count string or a map:

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
worker-templates:
  # A count string
  nightly: 10 linux-large-js

  # A fixed count with task rules
  release:
    workers: 2 linux-medium-js; 1 linux-large-js
    stop-after: [build]

  # Sized by the work in each run
  pr:
    small: 2 linux-medium-js
    medium: 3 linux-medium-js; 1 linux-large-js
    large: 8 linux-medium-js; 4 linux-large-js
    fail-after: 1
```

A count string lists `<count> <launch-template>` pairs separated by `;`, with at most 20 templates.
The template names can come from your [`launch-templates`](#launch-templates) or from the [pre-built templates](/docs/kb/launch-templates).

On your own CI provider, Nx Cloud has no job to take a machine setup from, so every count needs a template name.
A bare count such as `3` only works on Nx Cloud CI, where it reuses the machine setup of the job.

### `workers`

A fixed count of workers, all started with the run.
Can't be combined with `small`, `medium`, and `large`.

### `small`, `medium`, and `large`

Worker counts sized by the work the run has to do.
All three are required.

- `small` starts with the run, every time.
- At each distributed command, Nx Cloud compares the remaining work, after cache hits, with the stored timings of your workspace and picks a size. With no stored timings yet, as in a new workspace, every command picks `large`.
- Counts are totals per template, so picking a larger size adds the difference. A template's count can't go down from one size to the next, and workers are never removed.

By default, `small` covers up to 33% of the stored task time, `medium` up to 66%, and `large` the rest.
`small` and `medium` can set their own upper limit, a whole percent from 1% to 99%.
`small`'s limit must be below `medium`'s, and a size without `workload` keeps its default:

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
worker-templates:
  pr:
    small: { count: 2 linux-medium-js, workload: 10% }
    medium: 4 linux-medium-js
    large: 6 linux-medium-js
```

### `assignment-rules`

Rules that send matching tasks to specific launch templates.
For the rule format, see [assignment rules](/docs/kb/assignment-rules).

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
worker-templates:
  default:
    workers: 3 linux-medium-js; 1 linux-large-js
    assignment-rules:
      - targets: [e2e-ci*]
        run-on:
          - agent: linux-large-js
            parallelism: 1
```

### `scheduling-constraints`

Tasks that must never run on the same worker at the same time, for example because they share a port or a database.
Each entry selects tasks by `projects`, `targets`, or `configurations` globs, and `cannot-run-with` lists the tasks they can't overlap with on one worker.

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
worker-templates:
  default:
    workers: 3 linux-large-js
    scheduling-constraints:
      # e2e tasks share a resource, so never run two of them on one worker at once
      - targets: ['e2e*']
        cannot-run-with:
          - targets: ['e2e*']
```

Tasks outside the constraint still share the worker with them.
To give a task a worker to itself, set `parallelism: false` on its target in [project configuration](/docs/reference/project-configuration).

### `stop-after`

Targets after which workers shut down: once a command running each listed target has started, workers stop when they have no tasks left.
Include at least one target from each Nx command your pipeline runs.

Type: array of strings.

### `fail-after`

Number of task failures after which the run is marked failed.
`0` means never fail, so the run always finishes. `1` stops on the first failure.

Type: `integer`, minimum `0`. Default: `1`.

### `main-job-worker`

Runs distributed tasks on a background worker on the machine of the main job.
On Nx Cloud CI, it's on by default and uses the CPU and memory it detects on the machine.
Set `cpu` and `memory` to have it plan for only part of the machine, for example to leave room for services the job starts, or set `main-job-worker: false` to run tasks only on the other workers.
They don't change the machine's size, which comes from the job's `runs-on` launch template.
With [dynamic task packing](/docs/features/ci-features/distribute-task-execution#packing-budgets), it runs tasks together up to these values times the packing budgets.
On your own CI provider, `start-workers` doesn't start this worker from `ci.yaml`.
To send tasks to it, use `agent: main` in [`assignment-rules`](#assignment-rules).
The name `main` is reserved, so a launch template can't use it.

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
worker-templates:
  default:
    workers: 4 linux-large-js
    main-job-worker:
      cpu: 3
      memory: 6GB
```

## `launch-templates`

Machine setups, keyed by the name that count strings and `runs-on` refer to.
For the template format, see [launch templates](/docs/kb/launch-templates).

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
launch-templates:
  linux-custom:
    resource-class: docker_linux_amd64/medium
    image: ubuntu22.04-node24.14-v1
    init-steps:
      - name: Checkout
        uses: nrwl/nx-cloud-workflows/v6/workflow-steps/checkout/main.yaml
      - name: Install node modules
        uses: nrwl/nx-cloud-workflows/v6/workflow-steps/install-node-modules/main.yaml
```

Nx Cloud looks up a template name in `ci.yaml` and the files it imports, then in `.nx/workflows`, then in the public `nrwl/nx-cloud-workflows` templates.

## `ultracache`

Paths that [Ultracache](/docs/features/ci-features/remote-cache#ultracache) ignores when it records what a task reads and writes.

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
ultracache:
  ignore-reads: ['dist/target/**']
  ignore-writes: ['dist/target/**']
  task-exclusions:
    - project: app-e2e
      ignore-reads: ['apps/app/build/**']
```

Entries in `task-exclusions` select tasks by `project`, `target`, and `configuration`.
To exclude paths for a single target instead, see the [`ultracache` target property](/docs/reference/project-configuration#ultracache).

## `self-healing`

Overrides for [self-healing CI](/docs/features/ci-features/self-healing-ci#advanced-configuration-overrides).
On your own CI provider, these apply to runs started with `nx-cloud start-workers`.

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
self-healing:
  fix-tasks-patterns: ['*lint*', '*test*', '!*e2e*']
  auto-apply-patterns: ['lint*']
```

### `fix-tasks-patterns`

Glob patterns of task IDs that self-healing CI is allowed to attempt fixes for.

### `auto-apply-patterns`

Glob patterns of task IDs whose fixes are applied automatically without explicit approval.

## Import a section from another file

`launch-templates`, `ultracache`, and `worker-templates` can each be a path to another file.

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
launch-templates: .nx/pipelines/launch-templates.yaml
ultracache: .nx/pipelines/ultracache.yaml
worker-templates: .nx/pipelines/workers.yaml
```

Paths are relative to the repository root and read at the commit of the run.
The imported file holds only the content of the section, with no top-level key naming it, and can't import again.
A path must end in `.yaml` or `.yml`, can't contain `..`, and can only use letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `-`, and `/`.
A `launch-templates` file can also keep a top-level `launch-templates:` key, so an existing `.nx/workflows/agents.yaml` can be imported as it is.

## `on`

Named triggers that start the jobs.
Each trigger needs an `event` of `PR` or `PUSH`.

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
on:
  pull-requests:
    event: PR
    branches: [main]
    paths-ignore: ['docs/**']
  main-pushes:
    event: PUSH
    branches: [main]
```

Triggers can also filter with `branches-ignore` and `paths`, `PUSH` triggers with `tags` and `tags-ignore`, and `PR` triggers with `types`.
A positive filter can't be combined with its `-ignore` counterpart.

## `jobs`

The jobs of the pipeline, each on its own machine.
To convert a GitHub Actions workflow, see [move a GitHub Actions workflow to Nx Cloud CI](/docs/kb/migrate-from-github-actions).

| Key               | Description                                                                         |
| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `runs-on`         | Launch template the job runs on.                                                    |
| `resource-class`  | Machine size. Use with `image` and `init-steps` instead of `runs-on`.               |
| `image`           | Container image. Use with `resource-class` and `init-steps` instead of `runs-on`.   |
| `init-steps`      | Steps run once when the machine starts, such as checkout and install.               |
| `steps`           | Steps the job runs in order.                                                        |
| `workers`         | Workers that run the tasks of the job. `false` turns distribution off.              |
| `needs`           | Job or jobs that must finish before this job starts.                                |
| `if`              | Condition that must hold for the job to run. See [expressions](#expressions).       |
| `env`             | Environment variables for every step of the job. Takes [expressions](#expressions). |
| `defaults`        | Defaults for the steps of the job, such as `run.working-directory`.                 |
| `timeout-minutes` | Minutes before the job is stopped, at most 60.                                      |
| `permissions`     | Permissions of the repository token. See [`permissions`](#jobsjobpermissions).      |
| `name`            | Display name of the job.                                                            |

### `jobs.<job>.workers`

| Written as                                   | Means                                                                  |
| -------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `workers: 3`                                 | Three workers with the machine setup of the job, started with the job. |
| `workers: 10 linux-large-js`                 | A fixed count of a launch template, started with the job.              |
| `workers: { small: 3, medium: 6, large: 8 }` | [Sized by the work](#small-medium-and-large).                          |
| `workers: <name>`                            | An entry from `worker-templates`.                                      |
| No `workers`                                 | Only the main-job worker.                                              |
| `workers: false`                             | No distribution. Each Nx command runs its tasks on the job's machine.  |

Unless `workers` is `false`, the job also distributes tasks to its own [main-job worker](#main-job-worker).
Workers get the `env` of the file and the job.

The main-job worker starts after the init steps and before the `steps`:

- Init steps must install Node.js and the workspace dependencies. Otherwise, or with an Nx version too old to distribute, the job fails with a message.
- It keeps the environment it starts with. Set task variables in the `env` of the file, job, or [launch template](#launch-templates), not of a step.
- It sees files that steps write to the checkout.

Set `workers: false` on jobs that don't run Nx tasks, such as a deploy job.

### `jobs.<job>.steps`

A step runs a shell command with `run`, or a step template with `uses` and `with`.
It can also set `name`, `id`, `if`, `env`, `shell`, `working-directory`, `continue-on-error`, and `timeout-minutes`.

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: linux-medium-js
    steps:
      - name: Validate dependencies
        if: nxcloud.event_name == 'pull_request'
        run: node ./tools/validate-deps.mjs
      - name: Affected tasks
        run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
```

### `jobs.<job>.permissions`

A job's repository token is read-only by default.
Set `repository-contents: write` to let the job push commits and tags:

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/release.yaml
on:
  main-pushes:
    event: PUSH
    branches: [main]

jobs:
  release:
    runs-on: linux-medium-js
    permissions:
      repository-contents: write
    steps:
      - run: npx nx release --skip-publish
```

Put a job that sets this in a file whose only trigger is a push to a protected branch, like the one above.
An `if` in a file that also runs on pull requests doesn't protect it, because the job gets the write token when it starts, before `if` is checked.
See [keep secrets safe](#keep-secrets-safe).

- Only the job's own machine gets the write token. Its workers keep a read-only one.
- The token is `${{ secrets.NX_CLOUD_VCS_TOKEN }}` (or `${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}`), and the checkout uses it, so `git push` works without extra setup.
- It needs the Nx Cloud GitHub App. In a workspace connected another way, the job fails with a configuration error.
- Runs for pull requests from forks never get it. A job that sets it fails on a fork.

## Expressions

Nx Cloud CI evaluates `${{ <expression> }}` in these keys:

- `env` at the top of the file, on a job, and on a step.
- A step's `name`, `run`, and `with`.
- `if` on a job or a step, where the `${{ }}` around the expression is optional.

Launch templates also evaluate `${{ }}` in their `env`.
Other keys, such as `runs-on` and `workers`, take their value as written.

| Context   | Holds                                                                                                                                                                            |
| --------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `nxcloud` | `event_name`, `sha`, `ref`, `ref_name`, `repository`, `repository_owner`, `workspace`, `server_url`, `api_url`, and `is_fork`. `github` reads the same values.                   |
| `env`     | The `env` of the file and the job.                                                                                                                                               |
| `job`     | `status`, which is `success` or `failure`.                                                                                                                                       |
| `steps`   | `<id>.outcome`, `<id>.conclusion`, and `<id>.outputs` of each finished step.                                                                                                     |
| `needs`   | `<job>.result` of each job in `needs`.                                                                                                                                           |
| `runner`  | `os`, `arch`, `temp`, `tool_cache`, and `name`.                                                                                                                                  |
| `secrets` | Stored variables marked secret, and `GITHUB_TOKEN`, a repository token from the GitHub App. `NX_CLOUD_VCS_TOKEN` is the same token. See [keep secrets safe](#keep-secrets-safe). |
| `vars`    | The other stored variables.                                                                                                                                                      |
| `inputs`  | The inputs of a step template, inside that template.                                                                                                                             |

The functions are `contains`, `startsWith`, `endsWith`, `format`, `join`, `toJSON`, `fromJSON`, `hashFiles`, and the status functions `success()`, `failure()`, and `always()`.
`cancelled()` isn't supported.
An `if` without a status function runs only when the earlier steps succeeded, as if it started with `success() &&`.

### Operators

| Operator                         | Meaning                                                             |
| -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `( )`                            | Grouping                                                            |
| `.` and `[ ]`                    | Property and index access, such as `steps.build.outputs['version']` |
| `.*`                             | Every element, such as `needs.*.result`                             |
| `!`                              | Not                                                                 |
| `==`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` | Comparison                                                          |

`&&` (and) and `||` (or) return one of their operands, so `${{ vars.NPM_REGISTRY || 'https://registry.npmjs.org' }}` gives a default.

Literals are `null`, `true`, `false`, numbers, and strings in single quotes.
Write `''` for a quote inside a string.

- Strings compare without regard to case.
- Values of different types are converted to numbers before they're compared.
- `false`, `0`, `''`, `null`, and `NaN` are false. Every other value is true.

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
env:
  NPM_REGISTRY: ${{ vars.NPM_REGISTRY || 'https://registry.npmjs.org' }}
  CACHE_KEY: ${{ format('deps-{0}-{1}', runner.os, hashFiles('pnpm-lock.yaml')) }}

jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: linux-medium-js
    steps:
      - name: Read version
        id: version
        run: echo "value=$(node -p 'require(\"./package.json\").version')" >> "$NX_CLOUD_OUTPUT"

      - name: Lint
        id: lint
        continue-on-error: true
        run: npx nx affected -t lint

      # Only on pushes to main
      - name: Publish
        if: nxcloud.event_name == 'push' && nxcloud.ref_name == 'main'
        run: npx nx release publish --version ${{ steps.version.outputs.value }}

      # A leading ! needs ${{ }} or quotes, because YAML reads a bare ! as a tag
      - name: Upload coverage
        if: ${{ !nxcloud.is_fork }}
        run: npx codecov

      # Runs although Lint's failure was allowed
      - name: Report lint failures
        if: steps.lint.outcome == 'failure'
        run: node ./tools/report-lint.mjs

      # Runs only when an earlier step failed
      - name: Collect logs
        if: failure()
        run: tar czf logs.tgz dist/logs

      - name: Long-lived branches
        if: contains(fromJSON('["main", "next"]'), nxcloud.ref_name)
        run: npx nx affected -t e2e

  deploy:
    needs: main
    if: needs.main.result == 'success' && nxcloud.event_name == 'push'
    runs-on: linux-medium-js
    steps:
      - name: Deploy
        run: ./deploy.sh
```

With `continue-on-error`, a failed step has `outcome` set to `failure` and `conclusion` set to `success`.
On a pull request, `nxcloud.ref` is `refs/pull/<number>/merge`, so branch checks on `ref` or `ref_name` only make sense on `push`.

### `{{ }}` in older files

Files written for `.nx/workflows` may use `{{ secrets.NAME }}` or `{{ nxBranch }}`.
These still work, but use `${{ }}` in new files.

## Variables and files in each step

Every step runs with these variables:

| Variable                     | Value                                                       | GitHub Actions name       |
| ---------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- |
| `NX_CLOUD_CI`                | `true`, so scripts can tell they're running on Nx Cloud CI  | None, see below           |
| `NX_CLOUD_SHA`               | Commit being built                                          | `GITHUB_SHA`              |
| `NX_CLOUD_REF`               | Full ref, such as `refs/heads/main` or `refs/pull/12/merge` | `GITHUB_REF`              |
| `NX_CLOUD_REF_NAME`          | Short ref, such as `main`                                   | `GITHUB_REF_NAME`         |
| `NX_CLOUD_EVENT_NAME`        | `push` or `pull_request`                                    | `GITHUB_EVENT_NAME`       |
| `NX_CLOUD_REPOSITORY`        | `owner/repo`                                                | `GITHUB_REPOSITORY`       |
| `NX_CLOUD_REPOSITORY_OWNER`  | `owner`                                                     | `GITHUB_REPOSITORY_OWNER` |
| `NX_CLOUD_REPOSITORY_DIR`    | Directory the repository is checked out to                  | `GITHUB_WORKSPACE`        |
| `NX_CLOUD_VCS_SERVER_URL`    | Git host, such as `https://github.com`                      | `GITHUB_SERVER_URL`       |
| `NX_CLOUD_VCS_API_URL`       | Git host API, such as `https://api.github.com`              | `GITHUB_API_URL`          |
| `NX_CLOUD_RUNNER_OS`         | Operating system of the machine                             | `RUNNER_OS`               |
| `NX_CLOUD_RUNNER_ARCH`       | CPU architecture of the machine                             | `RUNNER_ARCH`             |
| `NX_CLOUD_RUNNER_TEMP`       | Temporary directory                                         | `RUNNER_TEMP`             |
| `NX_CLOUD_RUNNER_TOOL_CACHE` | Directory for cached tools                                  | `RUNNER_TOOL_CACHE`       |
| `NX_CLOUD_RUNNER_NAME`       | Name of the machine                                         | `RUNNER_NAME`             |

The same values are in the `nxcloud` and `runner` [expression](#expressions) contexts.
`GITHUB_ACTIONS` and `GITHUB_RUN_ID` aren't set, so tools don't detect the job as running in GitHub Actions.
Check `NX_CLOUD_CI` instead.

A step can write to these files to affect later steps:

| File                     | Write                | Effect                                                          | GitHub Actions name    |
| ------------------------ | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- |
| `$NX_CLOUD_ENV`          | `NAME=value`         | Sets `NAME` for the following steps of the job                  | `$GITHUB_ENV`          |
| `$NX_CLOUD_PATH`         | A directory per line | Adds it to the front of `PATH` for the following steps          | `$GITHUB_PATH`         |
| `$NX_CLOUD_OUTPUT`       | `name=value`         | Sets `steps.<id>.outputs.<name>`                                | `$GITHUB_OUTPUT`       |
| `$NX_CLOUD_STEP_SUMMARY` | Markdown             | Shown on the job's check, see [step summaries](#step-summaries) | `$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY` |
| `$NX_CLOUD_STATE`        | `name=value`         | State a step template reads back later                          | `$GITHUB_STATE`        |

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
steps:
  - name: Read version
    id: version
    run: echo "value=$(node -p 'require(\"./package.json\").version')" >> "$NX_CLOUD_OUTPUT"
  - name: Add tools to PATH
    run: echo "$HOME/.cargo/bin" >> "$NX_CLOUD_PATH"
  - name: Build
    run: npx nx run app:build --version=${{ steps.version.outputs.value }}
```

`$NX_CLOUD_ENV` takes one `NAME=value` per line.
For a multi-line value, write to `$GITHUB_ENV` with `NAME<<EOF`.

## Keep secrets safe

- **Pull requests from forks** get none of the variables from the **Environment variables** page and a read-only repository token. Self-healing CI doesn't run on them, and a job that sets `repository-contents: write` fails. If the author isn't an owner, member, collaborator, or earlier contributor of the repository, a member of your Nx Cloud workspace approves the run before it starts.
- **Pull requests from branches in your repository** get your secrets and run the code from that branch. Anyone who can push a branch can read them.
- **Secrets that can publish or deploy**, and [`repository-contents: write`](#jobsjobpermissions), belong in a pipeline file whose only trigger is a push to a protected branch.
- **`if` doesn't protect anything.** A pull request runs its own copy of `ci.yaml`, so it can change any condition. Use `if: ${{ !nxcloud.is_fork }}` only to skip steps that would fail without secrets:

```yaml
- name: Upload coverage
  if: ${{ !nxcloud.is_fork }}
  run: npx codecov --token "$CODECOV_TOKEN"
  env:
    CODECOV_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.CODECOV_TOKEN }}
```

On your own CI provider, [`start-workers --forward-secrets`](/docs/reference/nx-cloud-cli#nx-cloud-start-workers) forwards whatever the main job has, fork runs included, so your CI provider's settings decide whether forks get secrets.

## Checks on GitHub

Nx Cloud CI posts one `Nx Cloud: <name>` status for each pipeline file, and you can't turn it off while Nx Cloud CI is on.
Checks for each job, named `<name> / <job>`, are off by default.
Turn on **Show check runs for individual Nx Cloud CI jobs** in the VCS integration settings of your workspace to get them.
The **Re-run** button and step summaries both live on these per-job checks.
A configuration error in a pipeline file shows as a failed `<file> / configuration` check, with or without the setting.

### Re-run from GitHub

| GitHub button                      | What Nx Cloud CI does                                                                                       |
| ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Re-run** on a job's check        | Starts a new attempt of the pipeline file's run. Every job in that file runs again, not only that job.      |
| **Re-run all checks** on the suite | Starts a new attempt of each Nx Cloud CI run on the commit, except runs that failed on their configuration. |

The **Re-run** button is on per-job checks, so it needs **Show check runs for individual Nx Cloud CI jobs**.
A new attempt gets new checks, and the checks of earlier attempts stay as they are.
A run with a job that is still running can't be rerun, and pressing **Re-run** on it does nothing.

Rerunning needs the Nx Cloud GitHub App to receive the **Check run** and **Check suite** events.

### Step summaries

A step can write Markdown to `$NX_CLOUD_STEP_SUMMARY`, and `$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY` points to the same file.
When the job finishes, Nx Cloud CI joins the summaries of its steps in step order and shows them on the job's check under **Details**.
A job gets a check only with **Show check runs for individual Nx Cloud CI jobs** on.

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: linux-medium-js
    steps:
      - name: Affected tasks
        run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
      - name: Summarize affected projects
        run: |
          echo "## Affected projects" >> "$NX_CLOUD_STEP_SUMMARY"
          npx nx show projects --affected | sed 's/^/- /' >> "$NX_CLOUD_STEP_SUMMARY"
```

Step summaries work differently from GitHub Actions in these ways:

- The summary shows once the job finishes, not while it runs.
- A job's summary is capped at 65,535 characters, and a cut summary ends with `Step summary truncated.`
- Room goes to steps in the order they finish. When steps run in parallel, a later step can fill the limit and drop an earlier step's summary.
- Secrets are masked.
- `::error`, `::warning`, and `::notice` commands don't create annotations.

## Nx Cloud CI limits

Nx Cloud CI doesn't support these yet:

- Events other than push and pull request, including `schedule`, `workflow_dispatch`, and merge queues.
- `strategy.matrix`, `concurrency`, `services`, and job `outputs`.
- Actions from the GitHub Marketplace and `action.yml` files, including `actions/cache` and `actions/upload-artifact`. `uses` runs Nx Cloud step templates.
- OIDC identity tokens for cloud providers. Use secrets for now.
- macOS and Windows. Jobs run on Linux.
- Git LFS. The job image doesn't include it, so a repository that uses LFS checks out pointer files.

A job runs for at most 60 minutes, and that is also the default for `timeout-minutes`.
